gma_total": 7.18, "baseline_margin": 17.23, "analyst_adjustment": -0.23, "key_factors": ["All ten expert raters call the race Solid or Safe Democratic", "Prediction markets price Hickenlooper at 97-99 percent", "Hickenlooper has raised $8.1M to Baisley's $138K with no outside opposition", "Colorado's D+6 lean plus incumbency; no general-election polling available"], "rationale": "Colorado leans Democratic and Hickenlooper is a well-funded incumbent who easily fended off a primary challenge, while Republican Mark Baisley has raised almost nothing. Every expert rater and all three prediction markets treat the race as safely Democratic; independent Bob Chew's funded bid may trim the margin but is unlikely to change the winner.", "watch": "A well-funded independent campaign by Bob Chew or a late national environment shift could move the margin, though the likely winner is unlikely to change.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent John Hickenlooper (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Colorado's Senate race pits incumbent Democrat John Hickenlooper against Republican Mark Baisley. Hickenlooper fended off a progressive primary challenge from Julie Gonzales in June and enters the general election with a large resource advantage, having raised $8.1 million to Baisley's $138,000, with no outside groups spending against him. Colorado leans Democratic by about six points, and incumbency adds to that edge.\n\nAll ten expert ratings list the race as Solid or Safe Democratic, and prediction markets put Hickenlooper's chances between 97 and 99 percent. No general-election polls are available, so the outlook rests on fundamentals and ratings. Independent Bob Chew has raised $1.3 million and could draw votes from both major parties, a factor more likely to shape the margin than the outcome.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9917, "margin": 17.12}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0